orking people in  Britain to the front page of uh and the  front of all the news bulletins in the  country and we've woken up working  people to the fact that they're being  ripped off that they're underpaid and  their conditions are are under threat  right across our economy and right  across our society so that we've had a  game changer really across this year or  across 2022 in terms of what  working-class people are prepared to put  aren't we with and we've put the  government and corporate Britain Under  Pressure to give British workers a  square deal and give them value for  money for their labor  I mean do you get concerned though that  you might with all this disruption it's  the start of the new year you're going  to start alienating some of the public  with these tactics  well we don't want to alienate anyone we  want to run the railway service properly  we want a safe affordable Railway that's  running the interest of the people  rather than the profiteers the private  sector Railway companies that made  profit right through the covid pandemic  and every day during these strikes  they've made profits because the  government is subsidizing the strike so  we don't think we're losing public  support we're getting massive support  from people online in person on our  picket lines and even when our members  are at work on non-strike days the  traveling public is telling us they  support us they want a decent Railway  that's running their interests and they  don't want this inaction from the  government and profiteering from the  private sector Railway so as soon as we  can get a settlement and get the public  back on the railway and back to work and  using Leisure facilities to go about  their business the better for us and the  better for them yeah uh I mean on public  support ipsos did some polling that says  that there was less support for the  strikes and December than there was in  October so it does seem to be going down  a little bit but we'll just leave that  there for now I mean look there's a  payoff run on the table isn't there  there's a there's an offer for  um your conditions as well what more do  you want to see in terms of the offer  because as the transportation is saying  there that's there for you guys what  more do you want to see from them  well we the network rail offer there are  two different offers on the table the  network rail offer was rejected by our  members because of the conditions that  are on it it's not a terribly high value  uh payoffer so there's got to be an  improvement on the pay we're looking for  and we're looking for an agreement on  our conditions and we're looking for an  agreement on job security all of that is  achievable in the next few days the  companies know what they need to do and  they know what we could accept because  they're experienced people it's the  government that is stopping this happen  now I hope Mark Harper and you Merriman  can move this forward in the next few  days so that we can end this dispute  that we can put a set of proposals to  our members in referendums that they can  support but at the minute that's simply  not there our proposals and our needs  are quite modest from this this dispute  and if we can get towards a settlement  the happier we will be and our members  can get back to work you said an  agreement on job security I mean you've  got an agreement until 2025 on no  compulsory redundancies you want it to  go further than that  that's not particularly the issue the  length of the deal with network rail  it's what we've got to do we have to  accept a 50 cut in maintenance schedules  tasks on the railway the company wants  to introduce risk-based maintenance  rather than plan preventative  maintenance we think that's unsafe for  the public we think it's unsafe for our  people and it's not the way to run our  Railway on a more riskier basis than it  is there's also disruption to their  working lives and there are thousands of  job cuts on the table so we need a  settlement that addresses all of the  issues and not just one element  so if we can get that we can move this  forward as quickly as we can  and look there are talks come in as  we've heard and you said you wanted to  have talks with Mr Harper today if you  don't get what you want in these next  round of talks so you're going to step  up this action  well we've got a mandate that runs  through to May uh and we could probably  uh we would ballot again if we haven't  got a settlement we don't want to have  to do that we want to get our members  back to work so the quicker we can get  that the better but we have a mandate  for industrial election that runs on uh  all through the winter and all through  the spring and our members have voted  for industrial action twice and our  Network rail members have voted to turn  down the proposals that are on the table  so we need an improvement in the offer  so that we can get this thing sorted out  and if you don't get it what more days a  week out you're out four days this week  is it going to be seven days I mean that  next week where'd you take this to we've  got no action we've got no action uh on  the stocks at the moment we'll review  that next week when our members are back  at work uh and we'll consult our  Representatives around the country and  we'll decide what the next steps are  after that in the meantime I hope we can  get these discussions on if the  government is saying to me they want to  speak to us as quickly as possible we'll  deal with that before we set down any  more industrial action but we need to  get tangible improvements on what's been  proposed yeah you've made that point Mr  Lynch but I mean passengers who have  been disrupted on their commute back  into work this week people trying to get  around the country to see family and  stuff they'd like to try and know what  might be coming in the next couple of  weeks from Railway workers who are on  strike you're not going to tell us where  you might take this action you're  already out for four days as I said well  we haven't we haven't we haven't got a  schedule of uh further industrial  election at the moment what we do is we  put down a schedule of industrial action  we review what that what that's done  where the talks are and what our members  feel about the action that's taken so  after this action that finishes on  Saturday we'll review that early next  week along with any progress in the  talks that Mr Harper has  indicated he wants to achieve and we'll  see what needs to be done we don't have  a schedule set out six months in advance  we take it in stages and we'll do that  when we review where the dispute is next  week when when this phase is concluded  all right Mick lyncher from the rmt to  appreciate your time this morning thanks  for being with us
 

